WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.472
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.564 --> 00:00:06.230
LAURENCE MORONEY: So
welcome, everybody,

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.896
to this session, where I'm going
to talk a little bit about what

00:00:08.896 --> 00:00:10.880
to consider when
developing mobile apps.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.790
And I'm really happy
to hear the playlist

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:15.590
that they were
playing just before

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:16.760
as you were all coming in.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.664
Because I did a talk upstairs
in the previous slot,

00:00:19.664 --> 00:00:21.830
and the song that they
played was like, "What Do You

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:24.556
Do When Things Go Wrong?"

00:00:24.556 --> 00:00:26.180
And the microphone
failed and the clock

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.590
failed in the middle of my talk.

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:30.100
So thankfully, the song
they just played for you now

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.510
is "This Is What You Came For."

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:32.968
So thank you for
playing that song.

00:00:32.968 --> 00:00:34.707
So hopefully this is
what you came for.

00:00:34.707 --> 00:00:37.040
So I'm going to talk a little
bit about what to consider

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.910
when developing mobile apps.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.160
I'm a developer
advocate at Google.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.700
I work primarily on Firebase,
but I do some geo work,

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.630
and I do some
cloud work as well.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.230
And I want to talk a little bit
about some of the statistics--

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:51.422
oop, my slides are--

00:00:51.422 --> 00:00:53.130
so I want to talk a
little bit about some

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:57.080
of the statistics of developers
who are successful at building

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.450
apps and some of the habits of
highly successful developers.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:05.160
And the first statistic that
I'll show is this number, 51%.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.310
Some research has found
that 51% of developers,

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.130
as of the end of
2016, fall-- and this

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.140
is mobile developers--
fall below what

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.350
was termed the poverty line,
where the poverty line is

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:20.440
that their app makes
$500 or less per month.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.036
So 51% of developers are
falling into that bucket,

00:01:23.036 --> 00:01:25.410
and that's a lot of developers,
and that's a lot of apps.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.807
And once upon a time, there
felt like there was a gold rush

00:01:28.807 --> 00:01:31.140
for building mobile apps,
that you could build anything,

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.280
you could put it
in the App Store,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.738
you could put it
in the Play Store,

00:01:33.738 --> 00:01:35.400
and you could do
really, really well.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.760
But as of 2016, the majority,
51%, are not doing so well.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.760
29% of developers are above
the successful line, the highly

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.440
successful line, of making--

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.900
excuse me-- making
$5,000 or more per month

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.590
off of their applications.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:52.470
So that's a pretty
good number, 29%.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.920
I know I'd rather be in
that bucket than in the 51%.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.420
So what we've been
doing is we've

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.250
been doing a lot of
research into what

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.650
makes a successful
application, what

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.240
allows an application to be--

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.150
what allows an application
developer to be successful,

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.430
and what are some of the
habits that they have.

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.830
So breaking down those
numbers a little bit further

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.420
gives this bar chart.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.780
Now earlier, I mentioned 51%
are below the poverty line.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.870
But the vast majority of them,
that big bar on the left,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.090
are way below the poverty line.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:23.800
They're making less
than $100 per month.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.530
So it's about 39% are on that.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:29.280
About 12% are
between $100 to $500.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.650
But then, there's this
small bar over on the right.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.340
And these are the folks who are
making $25k or more per month

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.720
off of their applications.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.366
Now, what do they
do that I don't do?

00:02:38.366 --> 00:02:40.740
What do they do that the rest
of the folks here don't do?

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.495
What are some of their habits?

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:45.442
You know, one of them that we
took a look at is that 88%--

00:02:45.442 --> 00:02:50.790
whoops-- 88% of them are
targeting three platforms.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.430
They're targeting Android,
iOS, and mobile web.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.640
So the vast majority of the ones
who are extremely successful

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.410
are going beyond
a single platform.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.480
So like, it would be
point of advice number one

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.938
that, if you're
building something,

00:03:01.938 --> 00:03:03.570
don't focus on a
single platform.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.150
88% of the folks on the
right-hand side of this diagram

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:06.690
did not.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.180
They focused on Android,
iOS, and the web.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.339
And as a result, they're
in the $25k-plus club.

00:03:12.339 --> 00:03:14.130
There's a lot of other
things that they do.

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:17.060
So first of all, for
example, outsourcing--

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:18.810
a lot of the logic
that you would normally

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:20.310
have to build and
roll for yourself,

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.850
for example, authentication--
it's really, really difficult

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:27.090
to build a secure
authentication system.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.330
It's even more difficult
for you to maintain that,

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.965
because it's a
constantly moving target.

00:03:32.965 --> 00:03:36.150
There are new vulnerabilities
being found all the time.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:38.490
And if you build something
and roll it yourself,

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:41.527
you are beholden to your
developers and your SREs

00:03:41.527 --> 00:03:43.110
to be able to maintain
that while it's

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:47.460
under attack, whereas the highly
successful folks have moved

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.140
more into a federated identity.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.822
So you've probably
seen, most apps nowadays

00:03:51.822 --> 00:03:53.280
allow you to sign
in with Facebook,

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.950
or sign in with Google, or
sign in with Twitter, GitHub,

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:57.300
and other providers.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.810
So having that ability
to be able to offload

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.600
all of the work of building
and maintaining a security

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.910
infrastructure is something
that would then allow them--

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:09.210
if you were at the keynote
yesterday, one of the most

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.340
poignant things that
I remember hearing

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.470
was when Eric Schmidt was
talking about, you know,

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.060
the most difficult
thing to do is

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.769
to find the developers
to do the job

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:19.110
and to get your developers
focused on doing a job.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.029
Because most of you
are here because you're

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:22.212
software developers.

00:04:22.212 --> 00:04:23.670
And you're a really
precious asset.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.221
You're a really precious
resource to your company.

00:04:26.221 --> 00:04:28.470
And as a result, the folks
over on the right-hand side

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.750
here have had their
software developers

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:34.440
focused on building the business
value of the company as opposed

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.510
to building infrastructure
that would allow

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:39.690
them to run their applications.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:43.070
So with all of that in
mind, we built Firebase.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:44.850
Now, sometimes when
I talk about this--

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.580
and there's always somebody
in the audience that say,

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.520
wait a minute, you
didn't build Firebase.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:50.230
You bought it, all right?

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.564
And there's a little
bit of a joke in there.

00:04:52.564 --> 00:04:54.230
And there's a little
bit of truth there.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:56.364
Because you know, when
we acquired Firebase,

00:04:56.364 --> 00:04:58.280
we acquired some of the
technology at Firebase

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.863
like the realtime database that
I'm going to be talking about.

00:05:00.863 --> 00:05:03.770
But Firebase as a whole
goes way beyond just

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:06.050
the realtime database
and authentication.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:07.730
It's a suite of
products that we've

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:11.360
designed to allow you to
use your existing APIs,

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.560
be they iOS APIs, or Android
APIs, or JavaScript APIs,

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:19.070
to build apps, to build sites,
but to greatly enhance them

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:22.190
by having this suite of services
that makes it easier for you

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:25.161
to develop using things
such as a realtime database,

00:05:25.161 --> 00:05:27.410
using things such as the
functions that were mentioned

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:30.860
this morning, using things
such as, like, authentication

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.220
to be able to have all of that
available to you as a service.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.920
But then also, one of the
habits of the highly successful

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:38.420
developers that we
saw was that they

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.760
had a programmatic,
systematic way

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.950
of growing their application.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:45.200
They didn't just build
something, throw it

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.640
into the App Store,
do a marketing spend,

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.760
and then sit back and wait.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.820
So being able to grow
your application and have

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:55.580
technologies in place-- and I'll
talk about them a little bit--

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.329
that you can have to
grow your application

00:05:57.329 --> 00:05:58.460
is very, very important.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:00.950
And that's the second of
the pillars of Firebase.

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:02.970
The third of the pillars
of Firebase, of course,

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.052
is being able to earn.

00:06:04.052 --> 00:06:06.260
There's two ways that you
typically earn from an app.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.480
Number one is you charge
for it, or number two

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.170
is you put advertising in it.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:11.710
And as a result,
in Firebase, we've

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:15.530
brought the mobile advertising
APIs from Google into Firebase

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:17.600
to make it really,
really, super easy

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:19.360
for you to be able
to build these apps,

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:20.980
for you to be able
to grow these apps,

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.750
and for you to be able to earn
money from your apps and sites.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.970
And then finally, we bring that
all together with Analytics.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:29.420
The other habit of the
highly successful developers

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:31.979
was they know what
their users are doing.

00:06:31.979 --> 00:06:33.770
They're not just throwing
apps into a store

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:34.770
and hoping for the best.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:36.350
They know what their
users are doing.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:38.810
They're adapting to what
their users' requirements are

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:40.990
and the users' needs
are on the fly.

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:44.520
And all of that can be
driven off of Analytics.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.680
So importantly, and
one of the things

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.440
that we're announcing
at Cloud Next,

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:52.440
is that we're trying to bring
the ease of use of Firebase

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:55.590
together with the full range of
Google Platform, Google Cloud

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:57.210
Platform, services.

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:59.730
So if you're building
mobile applications,

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:02.070
we really think Firebase
is a great offering for you

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:05.460
to get started, for you to be
able to maybe build everything

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:06.210
that you need.

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:08.520
But if you start needing
the backend scalability

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.340
of the Google Cloud
Platform, then there

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.250
is a path for you to get there.

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:15.590
And a small side note is that
scalability is something,

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:17.340
to me, that's very
important, because it's

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:19.107
often miscommunicated.

00:07:19.107 --> 00:07:20.940
Because when you say
the word "scalability,"

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.950
you think being able
to go big and being

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:24.300
able to go big really quickly.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.070
And that's only
half of the truth.

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:27.990
The real power of the
Google Cloud Platform

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:30.390
is not just being able
to scale up very quickly,

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:33.120
it's also being able to
scale back down very quickly

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:35.902
and only pay for the compute
capacity that you're using.

00:07:35.902 --> 00:07:38.110
And there's one example of
this that I like to share.

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:41.430
It was a project that I worked
on when I first joined Google.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:43.830
And it was for a boy band, OK?

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:46.253
And anybody heard of the
boy band "One Direction?"

00:07:46.253 --> 00:07:46.752
Aah!

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:51.930
And what they did
was they put together

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:56.160
this online event on YouTube
for the release of a new album.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:58.290
And it was, like, a
seven-hour livestream.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:01.740
And during this seven-hour
livestream, every 10 minutes,

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.440
the band would ask somebody
to go over to an app,

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:06.570
to a second screen
application, and answer

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:09.459
a question so that they could
have the best-- they could find

00:08:09.459 --> 00:08:11.250
out who were the best
and who were the most

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:13.800
knowledgeable "One
Direction" fans.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:16.290
So think about that in
terms of scalability, right?

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:20.340
You have a boy band with
millions of fans and millions

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:23.820
of people watching a stream
and nobody looking at the app.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:25.800
And then the band
says, go to the app.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.066
What happens?

00:08:27.066 --> 00:08:28.440
Millions of people
go to the app.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:32.400
And your traffic goes from
nothing to massive spike.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:34.080
And then the people
answer the question.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:34.913
And what do they do?

00:08:34.913 --> 00:08:35.960
They close the app.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:37.625
So your traffic goes
back down again.

00:08:37.625 --> 00:08:39.750
And if you think about this
in terms of scalability

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.090
and capacity planning,
you know, typically, you

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:45.300
would build something that
maybe could handle this capacity

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.410
and hope that the spike
doesn't penetrate it.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:48.462
But you have nothing.

00:08:48.462 --> 00:08:49.170
You have a spike.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:49.672
You go down.

00:08:49.672 --> 00:08:50.380
You have nothing.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:51.088
You have a spike.

00:08:51.088 --> 00:08:51.780
You go down.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:54.060
But you're paying
for all of this space

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:56.047
below the capacity when
you're not using it.

00:08:56.047 --> 00:08:58.380
And one of the really neat
things about the Google Cloud

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:01.290
Platform and about App Engine,
which this was built on,

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.270
was that the number of
virtual machines that

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:08.130
were used to manage the site
followed this curve precisely.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.500
So when we ended up
finally shipping this thing

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:12.690
and running it, it
was massively used,

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:13.940
had a huge amount of traffic.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:15.810
And I'm not allowed
to share how much it

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:19.786
costs to actually run the App
Engine instances for this.

00:09:19.786 --> 00:09:22.410
But a few of us went out and had
a couple of drinks afterwards.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.201
And I don't even drink,
and the drinks bill

00:09:24.201 --> 00:09:26.130
was higher than the cloud bill.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:29.190
So if you manage scalability,
and you scale up,

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:31.550
and you scaled back
down effectively--

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:34.530
and that kind of scale that the
Google Cloud Platform gives you

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.940
is a very, very powerful tool.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:40.380
So coming back to
Firebase, what we're doing

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:43.110
is we're really bringing
these two services together.

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:45.330
We're bringing the
ease of development

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:46.860
and the ease of use of Firebase.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:49.650
But it's not a silo in
between the two of these.

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:51.510
You have a glide
path from A to B

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:53.200
if you need to get
that backend logic

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:55.960
and you need to build
that kind of scale.

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:57.960
So this is how we
typically look at Firebase.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.376
And here's how we like to talk
about it, the three pillars

00:10:00.376 --> 00:10:01.140
that I mentioned.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.810
So there are services to help
you develop your application--

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:06.270
a realtime database,
authentication,

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:09.240
cloud messaging, storage,
hosting, test lab, crash

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:10.640
reporting, and functions.

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:13.830
I'm going to talk about each of
these in this talk in detail.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:15.330
And then there are
the other pillars

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:17.460
that we're not going to
go into in a lot of detail

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:18.190
in this talk.

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:19.898
But if you have any
questions about them,

00:10:19.898 --> 00:10:21.160
come see me afterwards.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:23.580
And that's notifications
for growing your application

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:26.190
through re-engaging your users.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:27.780
There's a remote
configuration, which

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:29.071
is a personal favorite of mine.

00:10:29.071 --> 00:10:32.610
And that's a way that you can
store values, global values,

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:35.480
for your application on a server
and have them driven off of--

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:37.430
have them driven
off of Analytics.

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:41.160
There's app indexing where
users using the Google Search

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.320
app on their phone,
if they're searching

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.840
for content that's
in your app, they

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:47.310
should be able to find your app.

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:50.100
Because they've already made the
choice to download and install

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:53.130
your app instead of randomly
discovering it at some

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:54.570
point again in the future.

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:56.910
Dynamic links are
really smart links

00:10:56.910 --> 00:11:00.910
that will allow user A to
share content with user B.

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:04.260
And even the content
context can be taken

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:05.940
through an install session.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.380
Now, that's a bit of a mouthful.

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:09.300
So if I were to explain
that, it's like--

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.226
so for example, I
really like to eat,

00:11:11.226 --> 00:11:12.600
and my wife really
likes to cook.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:14.020
It's a great arrangement.

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:17.070
And if she discovers
some really interesting

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:20.940
recipes online and she wants
to send me these recipes--

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:23.700
and then she'll find, OK,
here's a recipe for X.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:25.192
And it's in this application.

00:11:25.192 --> 00:11:27.150
Why don't you take a look
at it and see if it's

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:28.440
something that you would like--

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:29.439
well, what happens then?

00:11:29.439 --> 00:11:31.260
She would probably
send me the recipe.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:33.750
And then I would go onto
the Play Store on my phone.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:35.040
And then I search.

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:37.830
You know, it's in Frank
Puff's cooking application,

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:40.230
so I start searching in
Frank's cooking application.

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:42.990
Frank's here in the front row,
so I'm giving him a hard time.

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:45.210
So I'd search Frank's
cooking application.

00:11:45.210 --> 00:11:46.140
And then I find it.

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:47.070
And then I install it.

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:48.486
And then that takes
a few minutes.

00:11:48.486 --> 00:11:50.040
And then it finishes installing.

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:51.000
And then I open it up.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.040
And what was the recipe again?

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:56.250
So that's the idea
of the dynamic links

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:58.100
was that if you had
sent me a dynamic link,

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:00.270
then the context of that
recipe would be maintained

00:12:00.270 --> 00:12:01.960
through my install session.

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:05.250
So again, as an app developer,
that's really useful for me,

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:07.170
because it will
help my app grow.

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:09.780
Because when person A shares
something with person B,

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:12.600
person B gets the context
throughout that entire session.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:14.670
And it makes for a
better experience.

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:16.590
App Invites is
another one of these.

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:19.230
It's like where friends help
friends discover great apps.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:21.540
I have an awesome
16-year-old son.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:23.910
And what my awesome
16-year-old son spends way too

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:25.950
much time doing is
cruising the Play Store

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.669
looking for great free games.

00:12:27.669 --> 00:12:29.460
And then when he finds
the good free games,

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:30.730
we will send them to me.

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:32.970
And because he's kind of
already curated them for me,

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:34.450
I know they're
going to be great.

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:36.810
And if an app developer
had built these games

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:39.450
using App Invites, as soon
as he hits the Share button,

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:42.120
I'm going to be at
the top of the list.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:44.070
Because the
intelligence behind this

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:46.664
says that, hey, when you
share, this guy, your dad,

00:12:46.664 --> 00:12:48.330
will take the game,
and will install it,

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:49.500
and will usually enjoy it.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:50.550
And of course, his
mom's going to be

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:52.633
at the bottom of the list,
because she will always

00:12:52.633 --> 00:12:55.380
say stop playing games,
stop playing too many games.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:57.802
And so all of these are
tied together by Analytics,

00:12:57.802 --> 00:12:59.010
and you can earn off of them.

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:00.760
But I want to focus
on the left-hand side,

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:02.505
the develop technologies.

00:13:02.505 --> 00:13:04.350
And so Analytics first--

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:07.320
the idea behind Analytics is--

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.165
I used to be a
development manager.

00:13:09.165 --> 00:13:11.040
And I don't know if many
of you have probably

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.331
sat in one of these meetings
where you've built an app,

00:13:13.331 --> 00:13:15.270
and you want to ship
the application.

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:17.370
And then your
marketing people say,

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:19.050
well, we want this
set of analytics

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:21.090
to see if our
campaigns are working.

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:23.580
And then the salespeople
say, well, we

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:25.237
want this set of
analytics to see

00:13:25.237 --> 00:13:26.820
if, you know, we're
meeting our goals,

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:28.440
if we're hitting
our commissions,

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:30.147
if the right people
are buying it.

00:13:30.147 --> 00:13:31.980
And the engineering
people are saying, well,

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:33.692
we want this set of
analytics, because we

00:13:33.692 --> 00:13:35.650
want to see the performance
of the application.

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:38.160
And we want to see if
we've optimized everything

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:40.860
that we need to optimize for
the best user experience.

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:42.600
And the CEO is probably
saying, well, I

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:45.450
want this set of analytics,
because this part of the app

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:47.160
was my pet project,
and I want everybody

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:49.599
to see this part of the app,
and all of these things.

00:13:49.599 --> 00:13:50.640
And then you usually sit.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:51.764
And you have to compromise.

00:13:51.764 --> 00:13:54.030
And what's the usual compromise?

00:13:54.030 --> 00:13:55.982
You've got to do
everything, right?

00:13:55.982 --> 00:13:57.690
And how many times
have-- have any of you

00:13:57.690 --> 00:13:59.960
ever sat in one
of those meetings?

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:01.387
Yeah, I see a few hands.

00:14:01.387 --> 00:14:02.970
And then you end up
saying, hey, look,

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:04.310
if I need to ship
everything, we're

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:06.480
not going to be able to ship
the application on time.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.470
You know, I can't put all
of these analytics in there,

00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:09.900
or we're not going
to be able to ship.

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:10.870
And it becomes ugly.

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:13.078
And there's compromises and
all those kinds of thing.

00:14:13.078 --> 00:14:15.750
So one of the nice things with
Firebase Analytics is that all

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:19.770
of the common event- and
user-centric things that can

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.530
happen in your application
are collected for you behind

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:24.360
the scenes for free--

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:27.810
not just monetary free, but
you don't write any code.

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:29.580
You link in the
Analytics libraries.

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:31.140
And all the possible
things you could

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:33.180
imagine, like opening
this activity,

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:35.940
closing that activity,
those types of things--

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:37.800
in-app purchases, all
that type of stuff--

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:39.599
is collected for you for free.

00:14:39.599 --> 00:14:40.890
So that's number one advantage.

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:42.180
Number two advantage
then, of course,

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:44.471
is that you can use the
analytics that you're gathering

00:14:44.471 --> 00:14:46.089
about your users
to drive behavior

00:14:46.089 --> 00:14:47.880
in some of the other
functions of Firebase.

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:50.650
And I'll talk through
that in just a moment.

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:52.650
So here's an example
that, last year,

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:56.120
for Google I/O, a Googler
built this Bingo Blast game.

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:59.540
And he put the
analytics into that.

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:02.610
And we just collected a whole
bunch of analytics from that

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:04.150
without writing any code.

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:05.816
And these are some
of the things that we

00:15:05.816 --> 00:15:06.930
were able to tell from it.

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:09.300
So I just grabbed this
screenshot from it.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:12.420
So for example, the one that I
always find much amusement from

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:15.550
is the amount of in-app
purchases there you can see.

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:17.940
And so in this case,
his Android application

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:20.820
had 22,700 monthly actives.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:22.560
And they spend $2,000.

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:25.610
And his iOS users, he
only had 5,000 of them,

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:28.020
and they spent
$3,000, all right?

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:30.450
So there's the old urban
legend that, you know,

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:32.164
the iOS users spend more money.

00:15:32.164 --> 00:15:33.080
But well, here you go.

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:34.920
Here was our analytics
to prove this.

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:37.500
And he was able to
gather this kind of intel

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:39.960
without spending a
lot of time coding.

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:41.460
Now, I mentioned
there's a lot of--

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:44.250
common events will be
captured for you for free.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:45.690
You don't write
any code for that.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:47.773
You're probably thinking,
well, what about a thing

00:15:47.773 --> 00:15:49.920
that my app does that
only my app does.

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:51.630
Maybe I'm building
a game, and I see

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:54.210
that everybody quits
the game after level 3

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:56.010
because the boss is
too difficult to beat.

00:15:56.010 --> 00:16:00.210
So that's at least a feedback
that I've gotten from my users.

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:02.370
So you could build a
custom analytic for that.

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:03.570
And unfortunately,
that does require

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:04.778
writing a little bit of code.

00:16:04.778 --> 00:16:07.140
We can't predict all of
that ahead of time for you.

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:08.790
But we've made the
APIs very, very easy

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:10.998
so that you can write that
code and build those kinds

00:16:10.998 --> 00:16:13.110
of analytics into it.

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:14.370
So on to the develop here.

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:16.410
First of all is the
realtime database.

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:20.670
Now, the realtime database is
a cloud-hosted NoSQL database.

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:22.552
It's a very, very
smart type of database

00:16:22.552 --> 00:16:24.510
so that you've got
synchronization and conflict

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:25.660
resolution.

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:28.080
So if you think about it, we
live in a multi-device world.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:31.620
Many of us have a phone, a
tablet, maybe a smartwatch,

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:32.120
a robot--

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:33.994
you know, these kind of
things that are going

00:16:33.994 --> 00:16:35.700
to be able to access our data.

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:37.890
Now, if we are building
an application that

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:40.290
requires access to the
data, or maybe on my phone,

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:41.910
I'm going to change
something, and I

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:44.270
need to see that updated
on my tablet, that's

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:46.340
the idea of how the
synchronization works

00:16:46.340 --> 00:16:48.820
and how the conflict resolution
works within Firebase.

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:50.780
This is all done for
you behind the scenes.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:52.880
It's an event-driven database.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:56.750
So when something happens, if I
make a change to a set of data,

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:58.120
an event will fire.

00:16:58.120 --> 00:16:59.870
The application running
on my other device

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:00.869
will receive that event.

00:17:00.869 --> 00:17:03.230
And then it can synchronize
its state to the data

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:04.710
based on receiving that.

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:06.680
And most importantly,
and sometimes overlooked,

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:08.869
is that you access it
directly from your app.

00:17:08.869 --> 00:17:11.150
So a lot of time, if
you're writing an app

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:13.250
or if you're building
something, you generally

00:17:13.250 --> 00:17:14.294
have to build a back end.

00:17:14.294 --> 00:17:16.460
And you have to write a lot
of code on the back end.

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:19.760
And more often than
not, you're simple CRUD,

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:22.339
simple CRUD operations where
you're reading, writing,

00:17:22.339 --> 00:17:24.670
updating, deleting data.

00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:27.560
And so you need to maintain
a back end for that.

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:29.574
And you need to build
a back end for that.

00:17:29.574 --> 00:17:31.490
And you need a totally
different set of skills

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:32.736
for building a back end.

00:17:32.736 --> 00:17:35.360
And one of the nice things about
the Firebase remote database--

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:37.626
or the realtime database--
is that this is just code

00:17:37.626 --> 00:17:38.750
that's running in your app.

00:17:38.750 --> 00:17:40.100
So you have the same code base.

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:42.960
And the Backend as a Service of
the Firebase realtime database

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:44.180
manages all of that for you.

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:46.790
So you've got that single code
base, that single set of skills

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:48.240
to make your life easier.

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:50.150
Now earlier, I
mentioned, of course,

00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:53.330
Firebase is working
very closely with Cloud.

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:56.297
And if you do want to
build a back end in Cloud

00:17:56.297 --> 00:17:58.130
and you want to get the
scalability of that,

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:01.276
or if you want to get the
logic, the programming logic

00:18:01.276 --> 00:18:02.650
that, sometimes,
you only do want

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:04.567
to put on a back
end, then of course,

00:18:04.567 --> 00:18:06.650
there are technologies to
make them work together.

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:08.300
And we'll be talking about
them a little bit later

00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:09.560
in this presentation.

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:12.820
But a clear use case for
that that I always see is--

00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:15.869
and I used to work in
the financial analytics,

00:18:15.869 --> 00:18:17.410
not to be confused
with the analytics

00:18:17.410 --> 00:18:18.890
I spoke about earlier on.

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:20.690
And a lot of
financial companies,

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:23.540
they have a set of analytics
for a particular company

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:25.040
that they can use
to measure and get

00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:26.660
a temperature for that company.

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:27.939
And that's their crown jewels.

00:18:27.939 --> 00:18:30.230
They're not going to distribute
that in an application.

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:31.771
They want to keep
that on a back end.

00:18:31.771 --> 00:18:33.695
They want to keep
that for themselves

00:18:33.695 --> 00:18:35.589
so that nobody else can
see those analytics.

00:18:35.589 --> 00:18:37.130
And you know, that's
a great use case

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:39.171
for where you may want to
put code on a back end,

00:18:39.171 --> 00:18:41.330
and not in Firebase, and
not on the front end.

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:43.710
So you've got the option
to be able to do both.

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:46.310
But when you're building with
the Firebase realtime database,

00:18:46.310 --> 00:18:48.101
you can do that from
within your client app

00:18:48.101 --> 00:18:50.851
without needing that
back end at all.

00:18:50.851 --> 00:18:52.350
And here's how
synchronization works

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:53.550
that I spoke about earlier on.

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:54.540
You've got multiple devices.

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:55.470
You've got one database.

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:57.344
And the synchronization
fans out between them

00:18:57.344 --> 00:19:01.495
because of how the database
is built to be event-driven.

00:19:01.495 --> 00:19:03.120
And another really
neat one is the fact

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:05.050
that it has offline access.

00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:06.600
So if you are building
an application

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:09.090
and you are using Firebase,
but you don't have connectivity

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:10.640
at this moment in
time, but you're

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:12.937
writing to that Firebase,
it will get cached.

00:19:12.937 --> 00:19:14.520
And then the next
time you come online

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:16.019
and you reconnect,
then the data can

00:19:16.019 --> 00:19:17.520
be synchronized between them.

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:20.950
So I was doing a talk earlier
on today about geolocation.

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:23.697
And I used this little
guy to demo geolocation.

00:19:23.697 --> 00:19:25.280
And one of the
questions that I always

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:27.404
get, if you doing geolocation
and you're storing it

00:19:27.404 --> 00:19:30.120
in Firebase, what happens when
you go into a tunnel, right?

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:31.500
And then you can't dial home.

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:32.970
And you can't store
your location.

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:34.380
And you can't get these details.

00:19:34.380 --> 00:19:36.046
And that's where
something like Firebase

00:19:36.046 --> 00:19:37.764
is a really good
solution for that.

00:19:37.764 --> 00:19:39.930
Because you're getting your
geolocation information.

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:40.830
You're storing it.

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:41.790
You're not connected.

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:42.710
You come out of the tunnel.

00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:43.660
You get reconnected.

00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:46.650
And the data can
get re-synchronized.

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:48.790
So then, there's authentication.

00:19:48.790 --> 00:19:50.710
User management and
authentication, of course,

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:51.850
is really important.

00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:53.890
If you want any kind
of custom experience

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:57.040
in your app for your end user,
even if it's just a high score

00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:59.320
table in a game or
something along those lines,

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:01.720
you need to be able
to identify your user.

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:04.480
And your user wants to
know that their identity

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:06.250
details are secure.

00:20:06.250 --> 00:20:09.010
They don't want to be able to
give tons of information to you

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:10.169
that you may not need.

00:20:10.169 --> 00:20:12.460
I mean, do they need your
social security number enable

00:20:12.460 --> 00:20:14.320
to generate a high score table?

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:15.310
Probably not.

00:20:15.310 --> 00:20:16.462
And so-- maybe they do.

00:20:16.462 --> 00:20:17.670
There might be hash in there.

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:18.760
I don't know.

00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:21.460
So the idea is that,
with authentication,

00:20:21.460 --> 00:20:24.550
we offer, in Firebase
Auth, a number

00:20:24.550 --> 00:20:26.890
of different federated
identity providers--

00:20:26.890 --> 00:20:29.860
Facebook, Twitter, GitHub,
and of course, Google.

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:32.650
And so if you want your user to
be able to sign in with them,

00:20:32.650 --> 00:20:35.200
then you can almost
effortlessly implement

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:36.670
that in your application.

00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:39.550
The user signs in, for example,
with their Twitter account.

00:20:39.550 --> 00:20:42.310
Twitter sends back
a token saying

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:45.670
that, yes, this is the
user Bob Smith or whatever.

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:48.310
So then you can customize
your application to them.

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:50.920
And then you, as a business, are
not gathering any information

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:53.119
from that user, which
is very user-friendly.

00:20:53.119 --> 00:20:55.660
Because how many times do you
want to fill out a form saying,

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:57.201
this is my name,
this is my password,

00:20:57.201 --> 00:20:59.744
this is my address, as an
end user, when you already

00:20:59.744 --> 00:21:01.660
have a Twitter account,
or a Facebook account,

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:03.740
or a Google account, or
something like that--

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:05.890
so again, to make it
very, very user-friendly.

00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:08.800
But of course, if you want to be
able to identify users yourself

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:10.800
and not federate out
to them, then there's

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:13.090
the email and password
authentication

00:21:13.090 --> 00:21:15.640
built in to the
Firebase Auth where

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:18.292
you would gather their
username and their password

00:21:18.292 --> 00:21:20.500
that they want to use to
sign in to your application.

00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:21.749
And then Firebase stores that.

00:21:21.749 --> 00:21:24.040
And then they can
sign in with Firebase.

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:27.370
But there's also a really
neat thing called--

00:21:27.370 --> 00:21:30.640
we have an open source
library for Firebase Auth

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:32.505
for handling user flows.

00:21:32.505 --> 00:21:33.880
Because if you
think about it, it

00:21:33.880 --> 00:21:35.724
can get really, really complex.

00:21:35.724 --> 00:21:37.390
You open up an
application, and you want

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:38.640
to sign in to the application.

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:40.120
What happens next?

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:41.390
Maybe you want to sign
in with a Google account.

00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:42.400
Well, there are
many of you who have

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:43.780
more than one Google account.

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:46.170
Which one are you going
to sign in with, right?

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:48.204
And so, OK-- or
maybe you want to--

00:21:48.204 --> 00:21:49.870
you don't have a
Google account, and you

00:21:49.870 --> 00:21:51.370
need to sign up for
a Google account

00:21:51.370 --> 00:21:53.870
in order to be able to sign
in with the application.

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:57.190
So how do you handle all of
that, or the same for Twitter

00:21:57.190 --> 00:21:58.370
and others like that?

00:21:58.370 --> 00:22:00.640
So there's all of these
very complex flows

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:02.650
that a user needs
to go through to be

00:22:02.650 --> 00:22:05.050
able to sign in to
your application

00:22:05.050 --> 00:22:06.920
or sign up for your application.

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:08.440
So we've open-sourced
the library

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:11.620
where we've put years of
research into optimizing these,

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:13.360
particularly for Google IDs.

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:15.610
And there's an open source
library for Firebase Auth--

00:22:15.610 --> 00:22:16.630
it's on GitHub--

00:22:16.630 --> 00:22:17.290
that you'll be able to do.

00:22:17.290 --> 00:22:19.150
And you can drop that
into your application.

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:22.600
And then all of those potential
have been implemented for you.

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:25.030
And again, thinking of the
habits of the highly successful

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:27.520
developers that
you're not writing

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:29.680
all of this code for yourself.

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:32.470
You're being able to take
upon what we've done,

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:34.780
the work that we've done
and that we've open-sourced,

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:37.900
to make life a whole lot
easier for your uses.

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:39.580
Cloud Storage for
Firebase-- if you're

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:43.060
building an application
that needs to store files,

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:45.080
then Cloud Storage is
really handy for you.

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:46.750
Maybe it's a social
media application,

00:22:46.750 --> 00:22:48.708
and you need to store
user pictures, or videos,

00:22:48.708 --> 00:22:49.780
of those kind of things.

00:22:49.780 --> 00:22:51.610
Instead of building
your own infrastructure

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:54.700
for hosting files and then
thinking of all the complex use

00:22:54.700 --> 00:22:58.502
cases, edge caching for
people around the world,

00:22:58.502 --> 00:23:00.460
or what happens if you're
storing a large file,

00:23:00.460 --> 00:23:02.001
and then the person's
downloading it,

00:23:02.001 --> 00:23:04.450
and their connection breaks,
as can happen with mobile,

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:06.575
as you walk between access
points and access point,

00:23:06.575 --> 00:23:08.866
all of these kind of things--
all of this kind of logic

00:23:08.866 --> 00:23:09.490
was built.

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:11.800
I think, Eric, again,
yesterday, in the keynote,

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:14.080
said, hey, we spent
$30 billion on building

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:15.179
this infrastructure.

00:23:15.179 --> 00:23:16.720
Why should you build
it for yourself?

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:19.930
And this is one of the benefits
of that Cloud infrastructure is

00:23:19.930 --> 00:23:22.960
that the Cloud Storage for
Firebase is built upon that.

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:24.380
So one of the
benefits of that is

00:23:24.380 --> 00:23:26.540
like, you get
disconnected-- what happens?

00:23:26.540 --> 00:23:28.570
So 2 gigabyte file,
you get disconnected

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:30.130
after 1.9 gigabytes--
do you want

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:31.360
to start downloading again?

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:32.560
No.

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:33.770
So there are APIs for that.

00:23:33.770 --> 00:23:35.020
There are Cloud APIs for that.

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:38.180
So you could have that kind
of file management built

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:41.185
in your Firebase applications,
built in for you.

00:23:41.185 --> 00:23:43.060
One of my personal
favorites-- and I geek out

00:23:43.060 --> 00:23:45.760
over this one a lot-- is
Firebase Cloud Messaging.

00:23:45.760 --> 00:23:47.014
So as of--

00:23:47.014 --> 00:23:47.680
I think it was--

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:50.100
November last year,
we announced that we

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:54.640
send over 1 trillion
messages using Firebase Cloud

00:23:54.640 --> 00:23:56.650
Messaging every week.

00:23:56.650 --> 00:23:59.260
And the vast majority
of those messages

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:01.030
are received by
connected devices

00:24:01.030 --> 00:24:03.440
in less than 500 milliseconds.

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:05.740
So there's a massive
infrastructure there

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:08.680
that you can use to send
realtime or near realtime

00:24:08.680 --> 00:24:11.810
messages for your users,
and it doesn't cost a dime.

00:24:11.810 --> 00:24:13.240
It's completely free of charge.

00:24:13.240 --> 00:24:14.790
It works cross-platform.

00:24:14.790 --> 00:24:15.592
So it works on iOS.

00:24:15.592 --> 00:24:16.425
It works on the web.

00:24:16.425 --> 00:24:17.494
It works on Android.

00:24:17.494 --> 00:24:18.910
And this is something
that you can

00:24:18.910 --> 00:24:20.950
use to drive user interaction.

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:23.050
Like, one partner
that we spoke with,

00:24:23.050 --> 00:24:26.860
it's a company that does
notifications for people

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:28.680
that use public transport.

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:30.610
And so this is kind
of handy where,

00:24:30.610 --> 00:24:33.280
like if you live in a city, and
you rely on public transport,

00:24:33.280 --> 00:24:35.740
and there are delays in
the public transport,

00:24:35.740 --> 00:24:38.230
then you can get notified
by this application

00:24:38.230 --> 00:24:40.540
that such and such a line
is closed down today or such

00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:42.679
and such a line is
on a half hour delay.

00:24:42.679 --> 00:24:45.220
But if you don't live in that
city and you still use the app,

00:24:45.220 --> 00:24:47.750
you don't want to receive
those notifications.

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:49.850
So there's intelligence
in the messaging platform.

00:24:49.850 --> 00:24:53.120
So notifications can be sent
for things such as topics.

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:54.640
So for example, if
I live in London

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:57.430
and I'm interested in
the London Underground,

00:24:57.430 --> 00:24:59.920
or if I live in New York and
I'm interested in the New York

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:02.725
subway, I should only be
receiving notifications

00:25:02.725 --> 00:25:05.350
about London if I live in London
and only receive notifications

00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:06.880
about New York if I live there.

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:09.250
So there's that versatile
messaging-- sorry,

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:11.980
that versatile way of
targeting your messages so

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:13.870
that the right users
get the message.

00:25:13.870 --> 00:25:16.060
And like I said, for
connected applications,

00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:18.242
it's usually less
than 500 milliseconds.

00:25:18.242 --> 00:25:20.200
And it's getting bigger,
and bigger, and bigger

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:20.741
all the time.

00:25:20.741 --> 00:25:21.820
And it's pretty cool.

00:25:21.820 --> 00:25:23.900
And it's totally free of charge.

00:25:23.900 --> 00:25:27.490
Remote Config I spoke a
little bit about earlier on.

00:25:27.490 --> 00:25:30.850
I find it really cool, because
it's server-side variables

00:25:30.850 --> 00:25:33.877
that you can modify without
redeploying your application.

00:25:33.877 --> 00:25:35.460
And they're integrated
with Analytics.

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:36.730
Let me give an example.

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:38.950
Say you build an
application that's

00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:40.770
an e-commerce application.

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:43.390
And as part of this, you
provide a standard discount

00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:45.140
to all of your users.

00:25:45.140 --> 00:25:46.630
So for the majority
of your users,

00:25:46.630 --> 00:25:49.030
the standard
discount might be 0%.

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:50.530
But some people--
like, maybe you

00:25:50.530 --> 00:25:52.840
want to grow the
application in Canada.

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:55.090
So you say, hey, for
people in Canada,

00:25:55.090 --> 00:25:57.250
we're going to give
a discount of 10%.

00:25:57.250 --> 00:25:59.920
And then, say you
realize that people

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:02.200
who make three
purchases in the app

00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:04.366
tend to make a lot more
after their third one.

00:26:04.366 --> 00:26:06.490
So people who have made
three purchases in the app,

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:08.880
we're going to give
them a 15% discount.

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:11.560
And then, let's
just say, OK, women

00:26:11.560 --> 00:26:13.970
tend to shop more in
this app than men do.

00:26:13.970 --> 00:26:16.270
So let's give women a
20% discount in this app.

00:26:16.270 --> 00:26:17.590
Now, think of all that logic.

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:20.417
Think of all that if this,
then that, if this, then that.

00:26:20.417 --> 00:26:22.000
And then you suddenly
realize that you

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:23.930
don't want to give the
Canadians that discount anymore.

00:26:23.930 --> 00:26:25.306
You want to change
it, all right?

00:26:25.306 --> 00:26:26.055
So what do you do?

00:26:26.055 --> 00:26:28.630
Do you ship a new app, and you
do all the if this, then that,

00:26:28.630 --> 00:26:30.400
and you do all the testing?

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:32.560
You could do that, but
that's a lot of work.

00:26:32.560 --> 00:26:35.300
With something like Remote
Config tied with Analytics,

00:26:35.300 --> 00:26:37.300
then each of those
conditions that I spoke about

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:38.889
can generate an audience.

00:26:38.889 --> 00:26:40.930
And then for that audience,
you can set the value

00:26:40.930 --> 00:26:42.310
to be a certain amount.

00:26:42.310 --> 00:26:44.080
For the Canadians,
it could be 10%,

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:46.900
for women, 20%,
those kind of things.

00:26:46.900 --> 00:26:50.270
And then when your app reads the
variable off of Remote Config,

00:26:50.270 --> 00:26:51.670
because it's driven
by Analytics,

00:26:51.670 --> 00:26:53.800
your app can just get
that variable and then

00:26:53.800 --> 00:26:55.464
can execute on that right away.

00:26:55.464 --> 00:26:57.130
And if you need to
change that variable,

00:26:57.130 --> 00:26:58.900
you change it in one
place on the server

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:00.890
without reshipping
your application.

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:02.402
So it opens up
really, really cool,

00:27:02.402 --> 00:27:04.360
really interesting
scenarios, particularly when

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:07.810
it's tied to Analytics, for
some good, fun stuff that you

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:09.850
can do with your applications.

00:27:09.850 --> 00:27:12.530
Another one, Test
Lab for Android, is--

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:13.910
OK, I'll do a quick survey.

00:27:13.910 --> 00:27:17.780
How many people here
have a Pixel XL?

00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:20.260
Oh wow, three?

00:27:20.260 --> 00:27:20.810
OK.

00:27:20.810 --> 00:27:23.090
I usually ask that,
and there's none.

00:27:23.090 --> 00:27:26.807
OK, so for those of you who
don't have a Pixel XL, how do

00:27:26.807 --> 00:27:28.890
you test your applications
if you're building them

00:27:28.890 --> 00:27:31.430
for a Pixel XL, right?

00:27:31.430 --> 00:27:33.920
The vast majority of the--
maybe 200 people in the room.

00:27:33.920 --> 00:27:35.390
So 97% and a--

00:27:35.390 --> 00:27:37.820
98.5% of us don't
have a Pixel XL.

00:27:37.820 --> 00:27:39.410
How do we test for that?

00:27:39.410 --> 00:27:42.560
Or what if you live
in a country where

00:27:42.560 --> 00:27:45.290
the Pixel XL isn't available
or particular devices aren't

00:27:45.290 --> 00:27:46.070
available?

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:48.260
Like maybe you
live in-- maybe you

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:50.180
build-- you're a
Chinese developer,

00:27:50.180 --> 00:27:52.040
but you're building for
the American market,

00:27:52.040 --> 00:27:54.859
and you don't have access to
American phones, or vice versa.

00:27:54.859 --> 00:27:56.400
What if they aren't
available to you?

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:57.024
What do you do?

00:27:57.024 --> 00:28:00.650
Do you create this huge farm of
devices so you can test them?

00:28:00.650 --> 00:28:03.560
So again, one of the habits
of the highly successful

00:28:03.560 --> 00:28:07.165
developers is being able to
test on a variety of devices.

00:28:07.165 --> 00:28:09.290
But it's really difficult
to send people on a plane

00:28:09.290 --> 00:28:11.040
to other parts of the
world to buy devices

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:12.380
that aren't available to you.

00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:14.540
So Test Lab for
Android is where we

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:19.040
have created a suite of both
virtual and physical machines

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:20.780
so that you, as an
Android developer,

00:28:20.780 --> 00:28:24.240
can upload your APK
of your application.

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:26.810
And you can have it tested
by a suite of robo-tests

00:28:26.810 --> 00:28:27.980
that we've designed.

00:28:27.980 --> 00:28:29.636
Or you can design
the actual test

00:28:29.636 --> 00:28:31.010
that you want to
have done on it.

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:34.100
And we'll send you back a
report on how it performed.

00:28:34.100 --> 00:28:35.770
And we'll send you
back screenshots

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:36.915
and those kind of things.

00:28:36.915 --> 00:28:39.350
And there's one really
fun, innovative use of this

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:41.050
that I heard about that--

00:28:41.050 --> 00:28:43.266
has anybody here
heard of Udacity?

00:28:43.266 --> 00:28:44.390
They're a training company.

00:28:44.390 --> 00:28:46.310
Yeah, cool-- so
quite a few of you.

00:28:46.310 --> 00:28:49.460
So for one of Udacity's
certifications,

00:28:49.460 --> 00:28:51.230
they have an exam
that they do for you

00:28:51.230 --> 00:28:52.820
to pass the certification.

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:55.280
And what they do is they give
you an incomplete Android

00:28:55.280 --> 00:28:56.360
application.

00:28:56.360 --> 00:28:58.880
And you have to fix the
bugs in the application.

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:01.100
And you have to finish
the application off.

00:29:01.100 --> 00:29:03.980
So when you do that, you
upload the APK to them.

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:05.930
They actually run
it through Test Lab.

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:08.180
So they've written a
suite of robo-tests.

00:29:08.180 --> 00:29:10.880
So instead of a human
looking at this application

00:29:10.880 --> 00:29:13.700
to say that Frank actually
finished this activity,

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:16.250
and he did it correctly,
and this button works--

00:29:16.250 --> 00:29:18.375
you know, those kind of
things-- that they actually

00:29:18.375 --> 00:29:20.250
have a robo-test to
do that for them.

00:29:20.250 --> 00:29:21.934
And to me, it a scenario that--

00:29:21.934 --> 00:29:24.600
I don't think we thought of that
when we were building Test Lab,

00:29:24.600 --> 00:29:24.890
right?

00:29:24.890 --> 00:29:26.810
And it's just how people
have innovated on it.

00:29:26.810 --> 00:29:30.080
I just find it's really
fun, and it's really cool.

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:32.180
Another one, Crash
Reporting-- the number one

00:29:32.180 --> 00:29:35.630
reason why you get bad reviews
in an App Store or a Play Store

00:29:35.630 --> 00:29:37.452
is your app crashes, all right?

00:29:37.452 --> 00:29:38.930
App crashing is a bad thing.

00:29:38.930 --> 00:29:40.942
But how do you fix it?

00:29:40.942 --> 00:29:42.650
I have this scenario
where there's an app

00:29:42.650 --> 00:29:46.410
that I was working on for a
show in Korea last summer.

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:49.940
And before I went to Korea, I
sent it to some people in Korea

00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:50.450
to test it.

00:29:50.450 --> 00:29:51.650
And it didn't work.

00:29:51.650 --> 00:29:54.370
And I'm like, OK, what
does "it didn't work" mean?

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:56.420
And they were like,
it didn't work.

00:29:56.420 --> 00:29:58.450
So how can I fix that?

00:29:58.450 --> 00:30:01.340
I mean, what's the
developer's usual answer?

00:30:01.340 --> 00:30:03.817
Works on my computer, you know?

00:30:03.817 --> 00:30:05.150
So it's like, how do I fix that?

00:30:05.150 --> 00:30:07.220
So with Crash
Reporting, thankfully,

00:30:07.220 --> 00:30:10.430
when I migrated the
application over to Firebase

00:30:10.430 --> 00:30:12.050
and used Firebase
Crash Reporting,

00:30:12.050 --> 00:30:15.200
then I was able to basically
get a detailed stack trace back

00:30:15.200 --> 00:30:16.574
every time it failed.

00:30:16.574 --> 00:30:17.990
And from the
detailed stack trace,

00:30:17.990 --> 00:30:20.600
I was able to figure out
why it was failing in Korea

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:22.310
and it wasn't failing
on my machine.

00:30:22.310 --> 00:30:24.440
And I was able to fix
it before I went there.

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:25.900
So when I was doing
the demo, I was

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:27.380
able to spend my time
preparing for the demo

00:30:27.380 --> 00:30:28.980
instead of trying to fix a bug--

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:30.000
you know, so those
kind of things.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:31.416
And of course--
and as you'll see,

00:30:31.416 --> 00:30:33.860
these three words,
"integrated with Analytics,"

00:30:33.860 --> 00:30:35.660
is built into almost anything.

00:30:35.660 --> 00:30:37.160
So as you're crash
reporting-- maybe

00:30:37.160 --> 00:30:40.240
you've got a million people
using it, and 100,000 of them

00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:41.330
are having crashes.

00:30:41.330 --> 00:30:43.400
By using Analytics, you
can slice and dice them

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:44.180
into audiences.

00:30:44.180 --> 00:30:46.850
So you can figure out which
audience is getting the crash.

00:30:46.850 --> 00:30:48.350
So then you can figure out why.

00:30:48.350 --> 00:30:50.433
And then you can fix it,
and deploy a new version,

00:30:50.433 --> 00:30:52.930
and hopefully stop
getting bad reviews.

00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:54.980
And then the other one
is Firebase Hosting.

00:30:54.980 --> 00:30:57.440
And this one makes me
smile a little bit.

00:30:57.440 --> 00:30:59.399
Because whenever I talk
about Firebase Hosting,

00:30:59.399 --> 00:31:00.773
a lot of people
think, eh, that's

00:31:00.773 --> 00:31:02.510
a little bit of an
afterthought, right?

00:31:02.510 --> 00:31:04.700
Because Firebase
Hosting, what it is is

00:31:04.700 --> 00:31:06.650
it allows you to
serve static assets

00:31:06.650 --> 00:31:09.450
like HTML, JavaScript, images.

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:11.150
It's Secure Sockets
Layer by default.

00:31:11.150 --> 00:31:14.279
So you can deliver a secure
site and save yourself

00:31:14.279 --> 00:31:16.070
all the hassle of
dealing with certificates

00:31:16.070 --> 00:31:17.194
and all that kind of thing.

00:31:17.194 --> 00:31:18.957
And you can map a
custom domain to it.

00:31:18.957 --> 00:31:20.540
But often, when I
speak to developers,

00:31:20.540 --> 00:31:22.748
they go, yeah, but I don't
really want a static site.

00:31:22.748 --> 00:31:25.100
You can't really do that
much with a static site.

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:28.124
So I wanted to come up with
a gratuitous demo that shows,

00:31:28.124 --> 00:31:29.540
maybe this is an
interesting thing

00:31:29.540 --> 00:31:31.576
that you can do
with a static site.

00:31:31.576 --> 00:31:33.200
So if we can switch
to my demo machine.

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:42.100
OK, so as a hobby,
I'm a CG artist.

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:43.850
And I'm a writer.

00:31:43.850 --> 00:31:45.680
And for one of my
books, when trying

00:31:45.680 --> 00:31:48.692
to sell it to a publisher,
they asked me to say, hey,

00:31:48.692 --> 00:31:50.150
can you send us
some visualizations

00:31:50.150 --> 00:31:52.150
of the characters, and
maybe what the characters

00:31:52.150 --> 00:31:54.290
look like so we can get
a feel for the scenario,

00:31:54.290 --> 00:31:56.165
we can get a feel for
some of the things that

00:31:56.165 --> 00:31:57.639
are going on in the story.

00:31:57.639 --> 00:31:59.930
So I've been doing that, and
I've been working with CG.

00:31:59.930 --> 00:32:02.150
So I thought, hmm,
Firebase Hosting-- maybe I

00:32:02.150 --> 00:32:03.360
can do something interesting.

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:06.860
So the biggest possible image
I can render was a 10,000

00:32:06.860 --> 00:32:08.560
by 10,000 image.

00:32:08.560 --> 00:32:11.480
So that's 100 million
pixels, all right?

00:32:11.480 --> 00:32:13.790
So those 100 million
pixels, I stored them

00:32:13.790 --> 00:32:14.950
on Firebase Storage--

00:32:14.950 --> 00:32:18.170
sorry, on a Firebase
Hosting site.

00:32:18.170 --> 00:32:20.580
And then the Firebase
Hosting site is at this URL,

00:32:20.580 --> 00:32:24.350
seadragontestsfd
06e.firebaseapp.com.

00:32:24.350 --> 00:32:25.894
And I'm on the conference Wi-Fi.

00:32:25.894 --> 00:32:27.560
How long do you think
it's going to take

00:32:27.560 --> 00:32:29.930
before you can see the image?

00:32:29.930 --> 00:32:32.870
Live demo-- drum roll--

00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:34.700
Frank looks worried.

00:32:34.700 --> 00:32:37.550
Drum roll.

00:32:37.550 --> 00:32:38.910
All right?

00:32:38.910 --> 00:32:40.540
OK, so I cheated.

00:32:40.540 --> 00:32:44.340
So I haven't downloaded all 100
million pixels of the image.

00:32:44.340 --> 00:32:46.830
So there was a JavaScript
library called Seadragon

00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:49.079
that I put and I put
onto Firebase Hosting.

00:32:49.079 --> 00:32:51.120
And with Seadragon, it
kind of works a little bit

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:53.280
like Google Maps that
you slice your image up

00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:56.197
into tiles, and tiles of
different zoom levels.

00:32:56.197 --> 00:32:57.780
So based on the zoom
level that you're

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:01.054
doing right now-- and in this
case, I'm zoomed right out.

00:33:01.054 --> 00:33:02.970
And in real terms, I'm
probably seeing, maybe,

00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:04.884
800 by 800 pixels.

00:33:04.884 --> 00:33:06.300
So it's only
downloading the tiles

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:07.270
that are appropriate for that.

00:33:07.270 --> 00:33:08.250
So I got it really quickly.

00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:09.840
But just to show
you that this really

00:33:09.840 --> 00:33:13.400
is a 100-megapixel image,
can you see her eye?

00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:16.105
All right, I'm going to zoom
in, and I'm going to zoom in,

00:33:16.105 --> 00:33:18.230
and I'm going to zoom in,
and I'm going to zoom in,

00:33:18.230 --> 00:33:20.220
and I'm going to zoom in,
and I'm going to zoom in,

00:33:20.220 --> 00:33:21.390
and I'm going to zoom in.

00:33:21.390 --> 00:33:23.595
So we can even see the
reflection in her eye,

00:33:23.595 --> 00:33:24.240
all right?

00:33:24.240 --> 00:33:26.430
So when I rendered this
image, I ray traced it.

00:33:26.430 --> 00:33:28.260
So whatever was behind
the virtual camera

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:30.960
got ray traced so that we could
see the reflection in her eye.

00:33:30.960 --> 00:33:33.330
And this is just
static JavaScript

00:33:33.330 --> 00:33:36.660
with a bunch of tiles
running in Firebase Hosting.

00:33:36.660 --> 00:33:39.150
And if I zoom out
again for a moment,

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:41.020
you can see individual
hairs in the eyelash

00:33:41.020 --> 00:33:41.978
and that kind of thing.

00:33:41.978 --> 00:33:44.459
So this really is a
100-megapixel image.

00:33:44.459 --> 00:33:46.500
And you can see these
monitors in the background.

00:33:46.500 --> 00:33:48.280
Well, you can't read
the text on them.

00:33:48.280 --> 00:33:50.431
But if I zoom in,
you can actually

00:33:50.431 --> 00:33:51.930
read the text on
them-- hopefully it

00:33:51.930 --> 00:33:53.305
shows up on the
projector-- where

00:33:53.305 --> 00:33:56.490
it says "comparative,
precious metals, 26 meters."

00:33:56.490 --> 00:33:58.960
Because that's like a scanner
on the ship behind her,

00:33:58.960 --> 00:33:59.760
that kind of thing.

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:03.019
So this is static hosting in
Firebase Hosting, you know?

00:34:03.019 --> 00:34:05.310
For something like this,
these are JavaScript libraries

00:34:05.310 --> 00:34:06.720
called OpenSeadragon.

00:34:06.720 --> 00:34:08.969
So I put the JavaScript
libraries on there.

00:34:08.969 --> 00:34:12.380
I put an HTML page calling
those JavaScript libraries.

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:15.719
I used a tool in Linux
to slice this image.

00:34:15.719 --> 00:34:19.100
So my 100-megapixel image got
sliced into about 8,000 tiles.

00:34:19.100 --> 00:34:21.960
With Firebase tools, I upload
all those tiles in one shot.

00:34:21.960 --> 00:34:24.177
And I have a website to
do something like this.

00:34:24.177 --> 00:34:26.010
So there's lots of
really, really cool stuff

00:34:26.010 --> 00:34:27.540
that you can do with
Firebase Hosting

00:34:27.540 --> 00:34:29.219
even though it's just
still static hosting.

00:34:29.219 --> 00:34:29.940
Don't overlook it.

00:34:29.940 --> 00:34:32.340
I think it's really cool, the
kind of things that you can do.

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:34.589
All right, so can we switch
back to the slides please?

00:34:36.830 --> 00:34:41.260
So today, we then announced
Cloud Functions for Firebase.

00:34:41.260 --> 00:34:44.757
And anybody excited
about Cloud Functions?

00:34:44.757 --> 00:34:45.340
Show of hands.

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:47.042
Whoo.

00:34:47.042 --> 00:34:48.750
So we've been working
really, really hard

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:49.749
on this for a long time.

00:34:49.749 --> 00:34:52.150
And it's been, in many ways,
the number one request.

00:34:52.150 --> 00:34:55.530
I mean, it's all very nice that
we can store data in Firebase.

00:34:55.530 --> 00:34:58.130
But can we do something with it?

00:34:58.130 --> 00:35:00.750
Can we do analytics on it?

00:35:00.750 --> 00:35:02.480
If, then, an event changes--

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:04.710
sorry, if a data item
changes-- from A to B,

00:35:04.710 --> 00:35:07.470
can we run some code
in response to that?

00:35:07.470 --> 00:35:10.200
Can we send a message from
Google Cloud Messaging when

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:11.732
value reaches a certain amount?

00:35:11.732 --> 00:35:14.190
All of these questions, that's
what Cloud Functions are all

00:35:14.190 --> 00:35:14.940
about.

00:35:14.940 --> 00:35:17.680
So you'll be hearing a lot more
about Cloud Functions today

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:18.880
and later today.

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:21.270
And I believe we have a
code lab downstairs for it.

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:23.560
Is that right, Frank?

00:35:23.560 --> 00:35:24.140
OK.

00:35:24.140 --> 00:35:26.480
We have a code lab for
it in the code labs area.

00:35:26.480 --> 00:35:28.490
So you can go and play with
it, and you can test with it.

00:35:28.490 --> 00:35:30.080
We've been so excited
by this, but we've

00:35:30.080 --> 00:35:32.240
been holding our tongue for
this event so that we can--

00:35:32.240 --> 00:35:34.500
you know, so you guys can go and
you can really play with this.

00:35:34.500 --> 00:35:35.180
And to me.

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:37.430
It's one of the things that
really brings your ability

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:40.640
to build with Firebase
to the next level.

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:44.240
When you start looking into
these, it's pretty cool stuff.

00:35:44.240 --> 00:35:46.610
So that's like the quick
whistlestop tour of what's

00:35:46.610 --> 00:35:48.170
available in Firebase.

00:35:48.170 --> 00:35:50.480
But like I've mentioned,
it's really, really important

00:35:50.480 --> 00:35:54.050
to think about Firebase
as part of Cloud overall.

00:35:54.050 --> 00:35:56.000
You know, Firebase
is our platform

00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:59.090
for building mobile applications
that work with Cloud.

00:35:59.090 --> 00:36:02.037
There are some other
sessions here about Cloud

00:36:02.037 --> 00:36:03.620
that you might be
interested in if you

00:36:03.620 --> 00:36:05.270
want to be a Cloud developer.

00:36:05.270 --> 00:36:08.360
But talking about Cloud,
some of the functions

00:36:08.360 --> 00:36:10.850
that are within Cloud, some
of the aspects of Cloud that

00:36:10.850 --> 00:36:12.320
are available to
you as a developer

00:36:12.320 --> 00:36:14.361
that, should you want to
migrate to the next step

00:36:14.361 --> 00:36:15.517
beyond Firebase--

00:36:15.517 --> 00:36:16.850
one of those is Cloud Functions.

00:36:16.850 --> 00:36:18.095
Does it sound familiar?

00:36:18.095 --> 00:36:18.705
All right?

00:36:18.705 --> 00:36:21.080
So it's very similar to the
Cloud Functions for Firebase.

00:36:21.080 --> 00:36:23.630
But this is Cloud Functions,
which are lightweight,

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:26.600
event-based microservices.

00:36:26.600 --> 00:36:28.616
Again, they're
completely serverless.

00:36:28.616 --> 00:36:29.990
So if you are
building stuff that

00:36:29.990 --> 00:36:31.520
runs in the cloud
on the back end,

00:36:31.520 --> 00:36:33.145
if you're building
stuff in App Engine,

00:36:33.145 --> 00:36:35.150
if you're building
stuff that uses Pub/Sub,

00:36:35.150 --> 00:36:37.160
if you're building
stuff that uses

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:40.790
the Google virtual machines
and Google Compute Engine,

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:42.500
then you've got
microservices that

00:36:42.500 --> 00:36:44.090
are event-based
that you can start

00:36:44.090 --> 00:36:46.550
using to orchestrate
these things to do

00:36:46.550 --> 00:36:47.630
interesting scenarios.

00:36:47.630 --> 00:36:50.840
And it's up to you to decide the
scenarios for your application.

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:53.494
But one of the nice things
about them is automatic scaling.

00:36:53.494 --> 00:36:55.910
Remember, earlier on, when I
mentioned the "One Direction"

00:36:55.910 --> 00:36:57.910
example, that that was
really, really cool where

00:36:57.910 --> 00:36:59.450
you can scale up quickly.

00:36:59.450 --> 00:37:01.150
But what, to me, made
that a special one

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:02.900
is that you could scale
back down quickly,

00:37:02.900 --> 00:37:04.760
and you're only paying
for what you use.

00:37:04.760 --> 00:37:07.121
That kind of automated
scaling, that kind of stuff

00:37:07.121 --> 00:37:09.620
is available to you if you're
using Cloud Functions to build

00:37:09.620 --> 00:37:11.200
your cloud applications.

00:37:11.200 --> 00:37:13.730
And open and familiar, and one
of the neat things about this

00:37:13.730 --> 00:37:15.521
if you're a developer,
is that you actually

00:37:15.521 --> 00:37:17.315
script these using JavaScript.

00:37:17.315 --> 00:37:19.880
And they run on
a Node.js engine.

00:37:19.880 --> 00:37:22.064
So if you want to
build, and test,

00:37:22.064 --> 00:37:23.480
and try and figure
out your script

00:37:23.480 --> 00:37:25.880
and figure out your logic before
you're connected to the cloud,

00:37:25.880 --> 00:37:27.620
before you're dealing
with cloud stuff,

00:37:27.620 --> 00:37:30.630
then you can do that
with the Cloud Functions.

00:37:30.630 --> 00:37:34.250
Node.js is just
JavaScript programming.

00:37:34.250 --> 00:37:35.359
Next up is App Engine.

00:37:35.359 --> 00:37:37.400
And the "One Direction"
example that I mentioned,

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:38.900
that was built on App Engine.

00:37:38.900 --> 00:37:41.540
And App Engine is a Platform
as a Service offering.

00:37:41.540 --> 00:37:43.790
So anybody familiar
with App Engine?

00:37:43.790 --> 00:37:45.260
OK, quite a few of you-- cool.

00:37:45.260 --> 00:37:48.140
And it's got a variety
of development languages

00:37:48.140 --> 00:37:51.020
you can use, Java, PHP,
Python, those kind of things,

00:37:51.020 --> 00:37:52.490
Go, of course, as well.

00:37:52.490 --> 00:37:54.320
And again, that has
the automatic scaling.

00:37:54.320 --> 00:37:57.680
So you think about it
as microinstances of VMs

00:37:57.680 --> 00:38:00.415
that don't have the capability
of a full virtual machine

00:38:00.415 --> 00:38:01.790
and operating
system, but they're

00:38:01.790 --> 00:38:04.786
programmable, which is what
makes them microinstances.

00:38:04.786 --> 00:38:06.660
And as a result, you
can create them quickly,

00:38:06.660 --> 00:38:08.240
and you can destroy
them quickly.

00:38:08.240 --> 00:38:10.490
And then you get all the
infrastructure benefits--

00:38:10.490 --> 00:38:12.400
Eric Schmidt $30
billion comment--

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:16.220
you know, the infrastructure
benefits like load balancing,

00:38:16.220 --> 00:38:18.650
health checking if you're
running them constantly,

00:38:18.650 --> 00:38:20.630
being able to log
their behavior.

00:38:20.630 --> 00:38:23.030
In the "One Direction"
example, logging really, really

00:38:23.030 --> 00:38:24.200
helped us early on.

00:38:24.200 --> 00:38:26.810
Because we couldn't figure
out why some of the things

00:38:26.810 --> 00:38:29.250
we were doing when we were
testing were going wrong.

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:31.250
And it turned out that
we had some fans who

00:38:31.250 --> 00:38:34.070
were such superfans of the
band that they figured out

00:38:34.070 --> 00:38:36.729
the URL of the site that we
were hosting the quiz on.

00:38:36.729 --> 00:38:38.270
And they were trying
to get in early.

00:38:38.270 --> 00:38:40.850
And they were breaking some of
our tests and stuff like that.

00:38:40.850 --> 00:38:42.710
And maybe we should have
used the fourth bullet point

00:38:42.710 --> 00:38:43.959
for them, and that's security.

00:38:43.959 --> 00:38:46.820
Because your instances,
your App Engine instances,

00:38:46.820 --> 00:38:47.911
are always secured.

00:38:47.911 --> 00:38:50.410
And of course, there's great
developer support, like I said.

00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:53.450
There's a multitude of
languages that it runs on.

00:38:53.450 --> 00:38:56.060
And they're pretty easy to
develop for and build for.

00:38:56.060 --> 00:38:58.610
Compute Engine,
then, is your ability

00:38:58.610 --> 00:39:01.130
to create a virtual machine
and run that in the cloud.

00:39:01.130 --> 00:39:03.014
And then you can scale
it out accordingly.

00:39:03.014 --> 00:39:05.180
One of the things I really
enjoy doing is-- you saw,

00:39:05.180 --> 00:39:08.432
my demo earlier on-- is
doing CG development.

00:39:08.432 --> 00:39:10.640
And sometimes if I'm going
to render an image-- like,

00:39:10.640 --> 00:39:12.212
that one wasn't photoreal.

00:39:12.212 --> 00:39:13.670
But sometimes when
I want to render

00:39:13.670 --> 00:39:16.400
an image, a photoreal image,
it can take 30 or 40 hours

00:39:16.400 --> 00:39:18.420
to render a big image.

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:20.060
But if I spin up
a virtual machine,

00:39:20.060 --> 00:39:22.640
upload my stuff to that,
get that to start rendering,

00:39:22.640 --> 00:39:25.000
then I'm not tying
up my home machines.

00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:28.190
And the machine that I usually
use at home to render it on

00:39:28.190 --> 00:39:31.610
runs an operating system that's
not on this silver machine.

00:39:31.610 --> 00:39:32.780
And it tends to crash a lot.

00:39:32.780 --> 00:39:34.770
So I won't say its name.

00:39:34.770 --> 00:39:36.480
So I like to upload
them to the cloud.

00:39:36.480 --> 00:39:39.950
And as a result, I
can fire and forget.

00:39:39.950 --> 00:39:42.320
I can put them up there,
render them, get an image back.

00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:44.390
So Compute Engine's really
handy for things like that.

00:39:44.390 --> 00:39:45.806
You can have Windows
in the cloud.

00:39:45.806 --> 00:39:48.740
You can have Linux in the
cloud, those kind of things.

00:39:48.740 --> 00:39:51.650
As Urs mentioned this
morning, how it charges,

00:39:51.650 --> 00:39:52.530
its minute-level.

00:39:52.530 --> 00:39:55.484
So you're not paying
excessive money for capacity

00:39:55.484 --> 00:39:56.400
that you're not using.

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:58.910
And as a result,
it's very flexible.

00:39:58.910 --> 00:40:02.242
Container Engine--
so I'll personally

00:40:02.242 --> 00:40:03.950
admit that I don't
use containers a lot--

00:40:03.950 --> 00:40:05.690
or I didn't until recently.

00:40:05.690 --> 00:40:08.240
And one of the really nice
things about Container

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:10.654
is that if you're using
full operating systems

00:40:10.654 --> 00:40:13.070
and you're running applications
on full operating systems,

00:40:13.070 --> 00:40:15.200
you can have a lot
of dependencies.

00:40:15.200 --> 00:40:17.390
And then, so if you try to
build an application that

00:40:17.390 --> 00:40:19.181
runs on one machine,
it doesn't necessarily

00:40:19.181 --> 00:40:20.390
run on another machine.

00:40:20.390 --> 00:40:22.580
So this little
guy, this robot, he

00:40:22.580 --> 00:40:25.800
runs on a Raspberry
Pi backplane.

00:40:25.800 --> 00:40:28.280
And so there's some
Python scripts on him

00:40:28.280 --> 00:40:31.010
that I use to turn him
into an autonomous robot,

00:40:31.010 --> 00:40:34.100
and to do geolocation, and
all that kind of stuff.

00:40:34.100 --> 00:40:36.470
But I have a Linux
machine at home

00:40:36.470 --> 00:40:38.630
that I use for
developing this stuff.

00:40:38.630 --> 00:40:41.240
And his Raspberry
Pi board uses Linux.

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:44.540
So I spent a lot of time
writing code, testing code,

00:40:44.540 --> 00:40:47.310
doing geolocation, make sure
things were working fine.

00:40:47.310 --> 00:40:49.310
And then I deployed it
to the Linux on that guy,

00:40:49.310 --> 00:40:51.200
and it didn't work, OK?

00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:54.100
Because there's a thing
in Linux called AWK.

00:40:54.100 --> 00:40:57.770
And the AWK flavor of the Linux
that the Raspberry Pi runs

00:40:57.770 --> 00:41:01.524
was very different from AWK
flavor on my desktop machine

00:41:01.524 --> 00:41:02.690
that I was using to develop.

00:41:02.690 --> 00:41:04.130
And I didn't know this.

00:41:04.130 --> 00:41:06.410
So I ended up having to
rewrite half of my code

00:41:06.410 --> 00:41:07.756
to get it to work on this guy.

00:41:07.756 --> 00:41:09.380
And that's one of
the things containers

00:41:09.380 --> 00:41:10.160
are really handy for.

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:12.260
Like for me, it was, I
have a development machine,

00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:13.580
and I have a single robot.

00:41:13.580 --> 00:41:16.970
But what if you have a cluster
farm of a thousand servers,

00:41:16.970 --> 00:41:19.490
and then it turns out that
those thousand servers, there's

00:41:19.490 --> 00:41:20.850
version drift between them?

00:41:20.850 --> 00:41:22.790
There's different
versions of, maybe,

00:41:22.790 --> 00:41:25.470
operating systems, or patches,
and all that kind of stuff.

00:41:25.470 --> 00:41:27.441
And that's the goal
behind containers,

00:41:27.441 --> 00:41:28.940
where the idea of
a container is you

00:41:28.940 --> 00:41:31.398
can create a script for what
an operating system's supposed

00:41:31.398 --> 00:41:32.000
to look like.

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:34.250
And then you can spin up
operating systems from that.

00:41:34.250 --> 00:41:36.860
You can spin up virtual machines
with those operating systems.

00:41:36.860 --> 00:41:38.609
And then you can have
that consistent set.

00:41:38.609 --> 00:41:40.401
For me, it was just a
case of a single one.

00:41:40.401 --> 00:41:42.108
But I can't imagine
what it would be like

00:41:42.108 --> 00:41:44.750
if I had had to make changes to
handle with thousands of them.

00:41:44.750 --> 00:41:46.700
And that's what Container
Engine's all about.

00:41:46.700 --> 00:41:49.520
It allows you to quickly
set up these clusters.

00:41:49.520 --> 00:41:51.560
You can manage
them declaratively.

00:41:51.560 --> 00:41:54.355
And that flexibility, if
you need to make a change,

00:41:54.355 --> 00:41:56.480
you could maybe make a
change to the entire cluster

00:41:56.480 --> 00:41:58.646
to bring in a new dependency
that you didn't realize

00:41:58.646 --> 00:42:00.450
that you had previously needed.

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:01.850
So these are some
of the services

00:42:01.850 --> 00:42:03.016
that are available on Cloud.

00:42:03.016 --> 00:42:04.640
And you, as a mobile
developer, can

00:42:04.640 --> 00:42:05.990
take advantage of all of these.

00:42:05.990 --> 00:42:08.660
And the thing that ties them
all together from the Cloud

00:42:08.660 --> 00:42:10.850
perspective is Cloud Endpoints.

00:42:10.850 --> 00:42:13.040
So Cloud Endpoints
allow you to build

00:42:13.040 --> 00:42:15.540
an API that wraps
your cloud services

00:42:15.540 --> 00:42:17.090
and then make that
API accessible

00:42:17.090 --> 00:42:19.020
to your mobile applications.

00:42:19.020 --> 00:42:21.320
So if you're running
stuff on App Engine,

00:42:21.320 --> 00:42:23.420
if you're running stuff
on containers, if--

00:42:23.420 --> 00:42:25.250
you know, the
Kubernetes engine, GKE,

00:42:25.250 --> 00:42:26.960
is what we use for
managing containers.

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:28.670
If you're running
your stuff in that,

00:42:28.670 --> 00:42:30.830
then the idea is you can
wrap them with an API

00:42:30.830 --> 00:42:32.810
that's callable from
a mobile back end--

00:42:32.810 --> 00:42:34.530
or from a mobile
application, sorry--

00:42:34.530 --> 00:42:36.660
so that then, these
become your back end.

00:42:36.660 --> 00:42:39.840
So if you're not using Firebase
for things like functions,

00:42:39.840 --> 00:42:41.600
but you're using
Cloud for functions,

00:42:41.600 --> 00:42:43.690
or you're building logic
that runs on App Engine,

00:42:43.690 --> 00:42:45.480
and you need to
easily be able to call

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:47.790
that from a mobile application,
Cloud Endpoints are

00:42:47.790 --> 00:42:48.800
your friend.

00:42:48.800 --> 00:42:50.990
They're a choose your own
framework kind of thing.

00:42:50.990 --> 00:42:54.180
So if you want to build
in Java for Android,

00:42:54.180 --> 00:42:57.232
or if you're building in
Objective-C or Swift for iOS,

00:42:57.232 --> 00:42:58.440
then you can call these guys.

00:42:58.440 --> 00:43:00.981
And like they say, they provide
that wrapping service for you

00:43:00.981 --> 00:43:03.970
to be able to build your
mobile applications.

00:43:03.970 --> 00:43:05.905
So that's a quick
tour of the things

00:43:05.905 --> 00:43:07.530
that you need to
consider when building

00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:09.690
mobile applications,
what we believe

00:43:09.690 --> 00:43:12.810
are the habits of highly
successful mobile developers,

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:15.510
why we've built Firebase
the way that we've built it

00:43:15.510 --> 00:43:18.390
with the services that we
have done so, and also,

00:43:18.390 --> 00:43:20.822
hopefully to show you that,
if you're going down Firebase

00:43:20.822 --> 00:43:22.530
and you're going down
the Firebase route,

00:43:22.530 --> 00:43:24.660
but you want to be able to
move to something bigger

00:43:24.660 --> 00:43:27.490
and scalable and have
logic on your back end,

00:43:27.490 --> 00:43:28.680
Cloud is available to you.

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:30.300
And as an application
developer in Firebase,

00:43:30.300 --> 00:43:31.550
you are also able to use that.

00:43:31.550 --> 00:43:33.407
So thank you very much.

00:43:33.407 --> 00:43:36.746
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:38.152 --> 00:43:40.610
And if we have any questions,
we can take them at the mics.

00:43:49.569 --> 00:43:51.110
AUDIENCE: Hello, I
have one question.

00:43:51.110 --> 00:43:53.690
Do you have any plans
on expanding Firebase

00:43:53.690 --> 00:43:56.079
to over-the-top solutions?

00:43:56.079 --> 00:43:58.370
LAURENCE MORONEY: When you
say over-the-top solutions--

00:43:58.370 --> 00:44:01.135
AUDIENCE: Apple TV, or
Android TV, or Roku.

00:44:01.135 --> 00:44:02.510
LAURENCE MORONEY:
I mean, I can't

00:44:02.510 --> 00:44:03.920
comment on any specific plans.

00:44:03.920 --> 00:44:07.280
But Firebase, of course,
has web-based interfaces.

00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:10.270
So with a web call, you can
read and write to the database.

00:44:10.270 --> 00:44:13.670
Not all of the functions
that I showed you have web.

00:44:13.670 --> 00:44:15.980
Like remote config doesn't
yet have a web interface.

00:44:15.980 --> 00:44:19.190
But on the whole, you
could make API calls

00:44:19.190 --> 00:44:20.937
in that way with web interfaces.

00:44:20.937 --> 00:44:21.770
AUDIENCE: All right.

00:44:21.770 --> 00:44:21.980
LAURENCE MORONEY: All right.

00:44:21.980 --> 00:44:22.646
AUDIENCE: Thanks

00:44:22.646 --> 00:44:24.925
LAURENCE MORONEY: Thanks

00:44:24.925 --> 00:44:25.840
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:44:25.840 --> 00:44:27.764
You mentioned the
Firebase Cloud Storage.

00:44:27.764 --> 00:44:28.680
LAURENCE MORONEY: Yep.

00:44:28.680 --> 00:44:30.680
AUDIENCE: Could you go
into a little more detail

00:44:30.680 --> 00:44:32.140
on where those
files actually live

00:44:32.140 --> 00:44:34.962
and what scopes are
involved to access those?

00:44:34.962 --> 00:44:35.920
LAURENCE MORONEY: Sure.

00:44:35.920 --> 00:44:38.470
So to the best of my knowledge--

00:44:38.470 --> 00:44:40.620
I don't really dive
down deep in them.

00:44:40.620 --> 00:44:42.402
But we have Google
Cloud Storage.

00:44:42.402 --> 00:44:44.110
And Google Cloud
Storage has data centers

00:44:44.110 --> 00:44:46.120
around the world and edge
caches around the world.

00:44:46.120 --> 00:44:47.200
And they're stored there.

00:44:47.200 --> 00:44:47.680
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:44:47.680 --> 00:44:49.846
LAURENCE MORONEY: So when
you're asking about scopes

00:44:49.846 --> 00:44:52.330
to access them, my
understanding would

00:44:52.330 --> 00:44:57.970
be that the scope that you allow
your user as a Firebase user

00:44:57.970 --> 00:44:59.860
is what would give them
the permission token

00:44:59.860 --> 00:45:02.920
to be able to access them
on the Cloud back end also.

00:45:02.920 --> 00:45:06.277
So if you've got a million
users of your application

00:45:06.277 --> 00:45:08.360
and you want all million
users of your application

00:45:08.360 --> 00:45:11.230
to see one set of files,
but a individual user

00:45:11.230 --> 00:45:12.910
only to see a
different set of files,

00:45:12.910 --> 00:45:14.650
that's done using
the Firebase Auth.

00:45:14.650 --> 00:45:17.274
And it works with the Cloud back
end to be able to manage that.

00:45:17.274 --> 00:45:20.136
And I see Frank nodding,
so I think I got it right.

00:45:20.136 --> 00:45:21.260
AUDIENCE: Great, thank you.

00:45:21.260 --> 00:45:24.160
LAURENCE MORONEY: He's my test.

00:45:24.160 --> 00:45:24.925
Over here.

00:45:24.925 --> 00:45:27.190
AUDIENCE: So currently,
on Firebase, I

00:45:27.190 --> 00:45:29.110
see there is a small
restriction, which

00:45:29.110 --> 00:45:32.710
is you can only log up
to 500 events and only up

00:45:32.710 --> 00:45:34.170
to 25 user properties.

00:45:34.170 --> 00:45:36.460
And they are not even deletable.

00:45:36.460 --> 00:45:39.540
So do you have any plans to
remove those restrictions?

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:40.895
LAURENCE MORONEY: Sorry, which
restrictions, specifically?

00:45:40.895 --> 00:45:41.478
I didn't hear.

00:45:41.478 --> 00:45:45.310
AUDIENCE: So for Analytics, you
can log only up to 500 events.

00:45:45.310 --> 00:45:47.590
And for remote config--

00:45:47.590 --> 00:45:51.220
and for user properties, sorry--
you can only have up to 25

00:45:51.220 --> 00:45:52.215
of them.

00:45:52.215 --> 00:45:53.090
LAURENCE MORONEY: OK.

00:45:53.090 --> 00:45:55.450
AUDIENCE: So are there
any plans to remove those?

00:45:55.450 --> 00:45:57.924
Because I feel like it's
easy to hit those limits.

00:45:57.924 --> 00:45:59.590
And especially with
the user properties,

00:45:59.590 --> 00:46:02.520
you can't delete them as of now.

00:46:02.520 --> 00:46:03.520
LAURENCE MORONEY: Right.

00:46:03.520 --> 00:46:05.630
We're not allowed to
speculate on future stuff,

00:46:05.630 --> 00:46:07.829
but we understand that
that's a requirement.

00:46:07.829 --> 00:46:10.120
And it's one of the requirements
that we're working on.

00:46:10.120 --> 00:46:12.864
And obviously, we have
Google I/O coming up

00:46:12.864 --> 00:46:13.780
in a couple of months.

00:46:13.780 --> 00:46:15.920
And we're working on some new
stuff for Firebase for that.

00:46:15.920 --> 00:46:17.920
So there may be some
new announcements then,

00:46:17.920 --> 00:46:20.830
but I can't really
speculate on a specific one.

00:46:20.830 --> 00:46:23.230
But understand that's it's
a big requirement that we've

00:46:23.230 --> 00:46:23.740
received.

00:46:23.740 --> 00:46:25.822
And we're taking it seriously.

00:46:25.822 --> 00:46:26.530
AUDIENCE: Thanks.

00:46:26.530 --> 00:46:27.696
LAURENCE MORONEY: Thank you.

00:46:32.430 --> 00:46:33.075
AUDIENCE: Hey.

00:46:33.075 --> 00:46:36.150
I just wanted to
ask you if Firebase

00:46:36.150 --> 00:46:39.460
has any plans of
incorporating A/B testing.

00:46:39.460 --> 00:46:40.710
LAURENCE MORONEY: A/B testing?

00:46:40.710 --> 00:46:43.200
Absolutely, via remote config.

00:46:43.200 --> 00:46:45.060
So the remote config
that I spoke about

00:46:45.060 --> 00:46:47.260
is perfect for A/B testing.

00:46:47.260 --> 00:46:50.910
So again, thinking about,
it's a server-side variable

00:46:50.910 --> 00:46:52.710
that's driven by Analytics.

00:46:52.710 --> 00:46:55.445
So you could say, OK,
let's set the variable to--

00:46:55.445 --> 00:46:56.820
I don't know--
for the background

00:46:56.820 --> 00:46:58.860
color of the main activity.

00:46:58.860 --> 00:47:01.350
That's going to be red
for people in Canada

00:47:01.350 --> 00:47:02.910
and blue for people in the US.

00:47:02.910 --> 00:47:04.980
And then you can perform
an A/B test like that.

00:47:04.980 --> 00:47:06.605
And then, using
Analytics, you can also

00:47:06.605 --> 00:47:10.510
measure how people have
interacted with it.

00:47:10.510 --> 00:47:13.560
AUDIENCE: How would we know
which particular cohort--

00:47:13.560 --> 00:47:16.200
I mean, which
particular, let's say,

00:47:16.200 --> 00:47:19.740
conversion corresponds to
which particular cohort that's

00:47:19.740 --> 00:47:22.540
been set in remote config?

00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:25.126
LAURENCE MORONEY: So--

00:47:25.126 --> 00:47:25.960
I'm trying to think.

00:47:25.960 --> 00:47:31.180
So if you define an audience in
Analytics and you specify that,

00:47:31.180 --> 00:47:33.880
again, just using the flippant
example of the background color

00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:36.775
of an activity as
an example, then you

00:47:36.775 --> 00:47:40.780
know that audience A has red
and audience B has green.

00:47:40.780 --> 00:47:43.570
So then, when you are-- you
can set another analytic

00:47:43.570 --> 00:47:45.160
on the application
that says, people

00:47:45.160 --> 00:47:48.490
who have a red background, are
they doing favorable activity

00:47:48.490 --> 00:47:50.680
like spending time
in the application,

00:47:50.680 --> 00:47:53.950
or doing in-app purchases
on the application,

00:47:53.950 --> 00:47:54.980
or something like that.

00:47:54.980 --> 00:47:55.570
And then measure that.

00:47:55.570 --> 00:47:57.680
And you could correlate
the two against each other.

00:47:57.680 --> 00:47:59.513
It's the only way I
could think of right now

00:47:59.513 --> 00:48:00.619
to do something like that.

00:48:00.619 --> 00:48:02.410
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I just
wanted to figure out

00:48:02.410 --> 00:48:06.421
a way to connect both
the things, so you know.

00:48:06.421 --> 00:48:08.170
LAURENCE MORONEY: You
could connect it off

00:48:08.170 --> 00:48:09.420
of the remote config variable.

00:48:09.420 --> 00:48:12.790
Because you know that audience
A has remote config variable

00:48:12.790 --> 00:48:15.430
red, and audience B has blue.

00:48:15.430 --> 00:48:16.980
So then you could
say, OK, when I

00:48:16.980 --> 00:48:21.730
got red, who spent time
in the application on red,

00:48:21.730 --> 00:48:23.554
or who did an in-app
purchase on red.

00:48:23.554 --> 00:48:25.720
And then you could create
a custom analytic for that

00:48:25.720 --> 00:48:26.909
and then measure that.

00:48:26.909 --> 00:48:28.450
So it's almost like
the remote config

00:48:28.450 --> 00:48:30.491
variable is the integration
point between the two

00:48:30.491 --> 00:48:31.670
audiences.

00:48:31.670 --> 00:48:32.670
AUDIENCE: OK, thank you.

00:48:32.670 --> 00:48:34.540
LAURENCE MORONEY: Thanks.

00:48:34.540 --> 00:48:35.140
Hi.

00:48:35.140 --> 00:48:36.970
AUDIENCE: Does the Test Lab--

00:48:36.970 --> 00:48:38.500
what testing does that do?

00:48:38.500 --> 00:48:41.560
I was looking for something
that does application response

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:43.180
time or performance testing.

00:48:43.180 --> 00:48:44.920
Does that test that as well?

00:48:44.920 --> 00:48:46.420
LAURENCE MORONEY:
I'm not sure if it

00:48:46.420 --> 00:48:47.860
measures actual performance.

00:48:47.860 --> 00:48:51.640
I think, like for common
things like the initial open,

00:48:51.640 --> 00:48:54.610
and initial close,
and quit, I think

00:48:54.610 --> 00:48:57.860
it reports back the time to
open and that kind of thing.

00:48:57.860 --> 00:49:00.610
But then, in-app performance
like moving from activity A

00:49:00.610 --> 00:49:03.222
to activity B, I don't think
it does that out of the box.

00:49:03.222 --> 00:49:05.430
But you might be able to
create a robo-test for that.

00:49:05.430 --> 00:49:05.950
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:49:05.950 --> 00:49:07.408
LAURENCE MORONEY:
My recommendation

00:49:07.408 --> 00:49:10.580
would be-- in the ask the
experts area downstairs,

00:49:10.580 --> 00:49:12.010
there's a guy called Doug.

00:49:12.010 --> 00:49:14.599
And he's our Test Lab guru.

00:49:14.599 --> 00:49:15.265
He's the expert.

00:49:15.265 --> 00:49:17.259
And he's been spending
a lot of time--

00:49:17.259 --> 00:49:18.550
guy with a little goatee beard.

